# `return x if cond` and `if c a b` are one question — whether a value is true
# or false — and for a while they were three answers. The interpreter said `a
# return condition`, native said `an if condition` because a guard lowers to
# the same `k_truthy`, and the page said `if takes a bool condition (got "7")`,
# quoted. The `if` token is in the source either way, so the guard borrows the
# sentence the other three sites already share.
pub fn opaque v
  v

pub fn pick n
  return "early" if opaque n
  "late"

pub play = print "{pick 7}"
